<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
<head>
  <title>CanviRes: Changing raster pixel side (multiples)</title>
<meta content="text/html; charset=ISO-8859-1" http-equiv="Content-Type"/></head>

<body>
   <!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent al titol de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<h1><span id="wpstitol"/></h1>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent titol de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->

  <p>This program changes a raster pixel side without changing its origin.
  Pixel side change can be done either by contraction or expansion of the
  original pixels. If you need to change the origin of the raster grid, please
  use the program <a href="DensRas.htm">DensRas</a>. At the end of this text
  there is a summary explaining when CanviRes should be used and when <a href="DensRas.htm">DensRas</a> should be used, including the options for each
  one.</p>

  <ul>
     <!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpsopcions"/>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->
  </ul>
  <!--In byte images the contraction criterion can be anyone of the three.<br>
In integer or real images the criterion can only be average or central value.<br>
In long images the criterion can only be the central value. -->

  <p>RECOMMENDATIONS for the EXPANSION:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Images of CATEGORICAL variables (thematic classes). The duplication
    method of CanviRes is adequate. If we wish an aesthetical improvement we
    can complete the process by using the methodology described in Xavier
    Pons' Ph D thesis, p. 213, paragraph 3.4.3. The complete reference is:

      <blockquote>
        Pons, X. (1993) &quot;Aportacions a la teledetecció i als sistemes
        d'informació geogràfica en estudis de vegetació&quot; Bellaterra.
        Servei de Publicacions de la Universitat Autònoma de Barcelona. 427 p.
        ISBN: 84-7929-647-X.
      </blockquote>
    </li>

    <li>Images of CONTINUOUS variables.

      <ul>
        <li>For sensor images we will use CanviRes if we do not want to
        corrupt the radiometry since we want to make a classification; this
        option will rarely interest us since it is more efficient to classify
        the image in its original pixel side (informative power) and then
        expand the result. We will use the mean or the program <a href="DensRas.htm">DensRas</a> (RECOMMENDED) when what we want to do is an
        expansion for visual interpretation.
        </li>

        <li>For digital elevation models the program <a href="DensRas.htm">DensRas</a> is the optimal solution.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>RECOMMENDATIONS for the CONTRACTION:

  <ul>
    <li>Images of CATEGORICAL variables (thematic classes). The Central and
    Mode methods are adequate, even though the Mode is RECOMMENDED, in
    general, for its superior performance in generalization.</li>

    <li>Images for CONTINUOUS variables. Any of the three methods of CanviRes
    can be used, even though the Mode does not usually have any interesting
    spatial significance.

      <ul>
        <li>For sensor images we will use the central value if we do not want
        to corrupt the radiometry (classification!) and will use the mean when
        we want to contract for a visual interpretation. Program <a href="DensRas.htm">DensRas</a> is not usually adequate since it only takes
        into account pixels immediately around the central value and not the
        whole window.
        </li>

        <li>For digital elevation models we will use the mean if we want that
        the value of each pixel informs us on the mean height of the whole
        cell and will use the program <a href="DensRas.htm">DensRas</a> if we
        want the value of the pixel to inform on the height of the central
        point of the cell (the method of the central value of CanviRes is
        nothing more than a particular case that <a href="DensRas.htm">DensRas</a> already takes into account when performing
        its calculations since this program applies exact interpolators).
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li>Images of all kind (CATEGORICAL or CONTINUOUS): We can use the Central
    value (using a value of CostatFinestra large enough) when we wish to
    perform a sampling avoiding spatial autocorrelation. Given the
    characteristics of the CanviRes module, sampling will be regularly
    distributed over the space.</li>
  </ul>RECOMMENDATIONS SUMMARY
  <pre>
EXPANSION:  + Categorical Map:   CanviRes [followed by the method in X.Pons' Ph D thesis]
            + Sensor image:      CanviRes for classification (not recommended)
                                 <a href="DensRas.htm">DensRas</a> for visual analysis
            + DEM:               <a href="DensRas.htm">DensRas</a>

CONTRACTION:+ Categorical Map:   CanviRes, M option
            + Sensor image:      CanviRes, C option for classification
                                 CanviRes, m option for visual analysis
            + MDE:               CanviRes, m option for mean heights
                                 <a href="DensRas.htm">DensRas</a>, for central heights
</pre>
  <hr/>

  <exclusiva>
